(project pricklypear-bumpers
 (doc "Bumpers: the FEEDBACK half of the bowling-bumpers design — lane sensors that fire while you work, not a rulebook you read up front. North star: one-shot features again. As the system grew, features stopped landing in one pass: pages come back unformed, budgets blow at render time, endpoints reach prod broken. This chapter owns the layered feedback that catches those classes at the moment of writing, cheapest tier first: (0) static effects at define time, (1) notice nudges on define/route via an out-of-band side-channel on the eval envelope, (2) on-demand dry-run verification of routes (unformed pages, broken endpoints, budget blowups become a REPL-visible report, not a browser symptom), (3) live telemetry pointers (/timing, function_stat, bench). The CONSTRUCTION half of bumpers — scaffolds, golden paths, generators, tool allowlists — stays in the habitat design inbox (notes slug agent-bowling-bumpers) until dogfooded (see borge/ai-authoring.borg drift-policy note in the note's graduation section). Mechanism/policy split with existing chapters: prim-seam owns the wire codec, lisp-interpreter owns eval semantics (UNCHANGED — notices are strictly out-of-band; define still returns what it returns), image owns route legality (route_gate), product-experience owns the human REPL rendering. This chapter owns the CONCEPT: what a notice is, when one may fire, the budget that keeps them from becoming noise, and the quiet-mode tiers.")
 (status planned)
 (* agent note (|2026-09-23 chapter created from discussion (roerick + pi). Origin: the habitat note agent-bowling-bumpers already catalogs the CONSTRUCTION-side bumpers (scaffold/crud generators, golden paths, allowlists, schema-as-data) and records "promote to borge only after dogfood." This chapter is the complementary FEEDBACK side, promoted directly to borge because its substrate is kernel (eval envelope, effect inference, route_gate) — the kernel already has every input the sensors need (effect inferencer, docstring parser, shared error codec), only the reporting surface is missing. Name dropped: 'areole' (botanical: the bump that bears the spines) — sounded wrong. Chapter name is the plain word from the note's vocabulary.|) *)

 (section notices-envelope
  (doc "Notices travel OUT OF BAND on the eval envelope: an additive \"notices\" field on the /api/eval JSON response (codec shared in common/ — the same codec prim-seam already factors so the image and proxy rims link ONE error<->JSON shape), a parallel JSON field on the lens local-eval path, and a muted rendering under the result in the browser /repl. define's RETURN VALUE NEVER CHANGES — any consumer reading \"value\" is untouched; old clients simply never see \"notices\". A notice is a small dict: {kind: define|route|tip|warn, name?, effects?, text}. Content kinds: define-notice (name + effect badge from the existing effect inferencer (lisp/lib/effect.ml), missing-docstring flag, bench pointer: (bench \"(my-fn ...)\" 100), spec-staleness flag when the binding's gen runs ahead of its spec section (record layer: tree-time; check semantics: habitat-workflow.gen-drift), prod follow-up pointer: /timing + function-stat); route-notice at route_gate publish time (path, handler, effect badge, htmx-harness pointer per docs/agent-ui-harness.md tier ladder, fragment-vs-shell classification); tip (short actionable hint); warn (rare — e.g. write-db effect on a public route). Generation splits by residence: notices computable from the define AST + effect inference are PURE and belong in lisp/lib so pp_eval, lens, and the line REPL emit identical text; branch-authority notices (doc coverage across the chain, bench history) are image-side enrichments only.")
  (implements "common (shared codec: notices field)" "image/lib (envelope assembly)" "lisp/lib (pure notice generation)" "cli/bin/pp_eval.ml (-q)" )
  (status implemented)
  (verify
   (build "dune build @all")
   (test "a define via /api/eval returns {\"ok\", \"value\", \"notices\": [...]}; a consumer parsing only \"value\" is unbroken")
   (test "pp_eval prints notices; pp_eval -q prints only the value; lens local eval emits identical pure-notice text for the same define AST")
   (smoke "browser /repl renders notices muted under the result; /api/eval response without a define carries no notices (empty or absent)")))

 (section notice-budget
  (doc "The channel dies if it nags. Hard rules: at most 2 notices per response; each tip kind fires at most once per session; a notice fires ONLY when actionable (missing docstring, strong effects, fragment violation — not on every well-formed quiet define); notices taper after the first N evals of a session (coach early, silent once fluent). QUIET MODE in three tiers, narrowest wins: (a) per-request {\"quiet\": true} (pp_eval -q, lens flag); (b) session taper (automatic); (c) durable per-identity preference via (agent-profile 'quiet) stored as a datum on <user>/personal. The human browser /repl defaults to the same budget as agent clients — no special-casing.")
  ; status partial 2026-09-23: bumpers M2 (loop 2; docs/plans/bumpers-m1-notices.md) implemented the at-most-2-per-response budget (Session drain cap), the once-per-tip-kind-per-session taper, and fire-only-when-actionable in the define-notice path. bumpers M6 (loop 8; docs/plans/bumpers-m1-notices.md M6) shipped tier-c — the durable per-identity quiet preference via (agent-profile 'quiet): forms () | ('quiet getter) | ('quiet #t|#f setter), registered inline in image/lib/session.ml make (beside analyze/explain, past the module-cycle boundary), persisted as a user datum type "profile" (author = the identity; mirrored data.u.<user>.profile.<id>; find_profile_datum / set_agent_profile_quiet / load_agent_profile + session_types.durable_quiet), loaded once per identity-session at registry make and consulted in api_eval as quiet = request-flag || durable (tier a > tier c; tier b is the automatic taper). Anonymous/public stay in-memory only. Still open to full: the taper-after-N-evals auto-mute.
  (status partial)
  (verify
   (test "replaying the same define N times in one session yields the docstring tip exactly once")
   (test "quiet request flag suppresses notices server-side (channel never decodes them), not client-side")))

 (section verify-route
  (doc "The dry-run tier — the one that buys back one-shot features. (verify-route \"/path\") invokes the handler once under the standard budget and returns a report dict: render cost (time, prim calls, eval-cost counter), effect set, and SHAPE classification: fragment (fits a glochid/habitat pane), full-shell page, or not-html-at-all. Unformed pages, broken endpoints, and budget-exceeded renders become a JSON result an agent can read before opening a browser or promoting. Known failure patterns become named warnings inside the report as they recur (first entry, from prod 2026-12-30: per-call allocation of large constants inside a render loop — the layout stylesheet lambda incident — warn when a handler rebuilds a big constant value per invocation where a delay-define would pay it once).")
  (implements "image/lib (verify_route prim, session.ml)" )
  ; status implemented 2026-09-23: bumpers M4 (loop 5; docs/plans/bumpers-m1-notices.md M4) shipped
  ; the (verify-route "/path") prim (image/lib/session.ml verify_route_fn, inline prim registration
  ; next to route/dispatch): resolves the handler on the session's chain, reports static
  ; {cost{ms,prim-calls,eval-cost}, effects badge, shape} + verify_route_warnings, then invokes
  ; the handler ONCE under a fresh standard budget (default 400k steps) with a default GET
  ; request bound so request-* prims resolve. Budget overflow is a REPORT FIELD
  ; {kind:"budget-exceeded"} — never an exception/500 (verified: an infinite 100M-count
  ; recursion reports eval-cost 400001 in-port and returns, not a hang). First named warning
  ; pattern implemented in lisp/lib/effect.ml (verify_route_warnings / has_large_const_on_path):
  ; a large literal constant (>=1024 chars or a (list ...) of >=24 literal strings fed to
  ; html/join) rebuilt per invocation -> "consider (delay ...)". Conservative by construction:
  ; only AST literals count, so request-dependent builders can never be flagged (false
  ; negatives acceptable, not false positives). The prim itself is classified WriteDb (same
  ; executor semantics as route/dispatch — it RUNS a named handler, so a public-route handler
  ; must never be able to call it and trigger another route's writes).
  (status implemented)
  (verify
   (smoke "(verify-route on a notes face) returns cost + effects + fragment classification as JSON")
   (test "a handler exceeding the standard budget surfaces as a report field with kind budget-exceeded, not as a 500 or a browser hang")))

 (section fragment-by-default
  (doc "App faces SHOULD be htmx fragments so glochid panes and the habitat shell can wear them (glochid.borg: fragment routes are the seam). This is a DEFAULT, not a gate: full-shell standalone pages are legitimate citizens (libs/layout neocities pages exist and stay). The lane sensor, not the wall: route-notices classify the handler's shape and remark on it (\"renders a full shell; apps fit panes as fragments built from libs/ui + libs/layout; standalone page? declare it\"), and a declaration (route annotation or package datum, e.g. standalone: true) SILENCES that notice family for it deliberately. (app-check \"pkg\") reports the fragment-fit of every route in a package — advisory classification output, never a publish block; route_gate stays about legality (handler exists, effects permitted), never about shape. Deliberate deviation is declared, never sneered at, so that quiet-mode is not the escape hatch people reach for when they exercise a legitimate feature.")
  ; status partial 2026-09-23: bumpers M3 (loop 4; docs/plans/bumpers-m1-notices.md M3) implemented the
  ; lane sensor: route-notices at route_gate classify the handler AST (Effect.classify_route_shape /
  ; expr_uses_head in lisp/lib/effect.ml over the gate's own Route_gate.handler_body walk) and emit
  ; {kind:route,name:path,effects:badge,text:remark} with the htmx-harness pointer (docs/agent-ui-harness.md
  ; tier ladder); a full-shell page gets the "renders a full shell … declare it" remark, a fragment gets
  ; the harness pointer, a plain/JSON endpoint just "published". Declared-standalone = a trailing 'standalone
  ; arg on (route …)/(public-route …) (route annotation branch of the declaration sentence) — session-scoped
  ; and monotone via session_types.standalone_routes; cross-session durability filed BORGE-1790180098
  ; (needs a route-meta schema change, which M3 forbids). route_gate legality UNCHANGED (no new denial
  ; cases). app-check shipped bumpers M5 (loop 7; docs/plans/bumpers-m1-notices.md M5):
  ; the (app-check "pkg") prim (image/lib/agent_tools.ml, registered in prims_code.ml) reports
  ; per-route fragment/shell/not-html classification (unbound = handler not defined yet) using the
  ; same Effect.classify_route_shape / Route_gate.handler_body walk as the route-notices — advisory
  ; only, route_gate stays about legality. Classified ReadDb (discovery, never writes).
  (status implemented)
  (verify
   (test "app-check classifies fragment vs shell vs not-html per route; a declared-standalone route draws no fragment notices in later sessions")
   (test "route_gate publishes a full-shell handler with identical legality rules as a fragment handler")))

 (section discovery-surfaces
  (doc "The reference side of the same channel — discovery prims that already exist (describe / apropos-docs / function-doc) gain the missing affordances: effect badges in their text output (read-db vs write-db visible in every listing — an agent learns a package's capability structure from ONE describe), a doc-coverage footer on package describe (\"documented 12/15 — (docs-missing \\\"pkg\\\")\"), and a (docs-missing [pkg]) prim listing visible bindings without docstrings. Error-occasion teaching rides the shared codec: known error classes carry an optional \"hint\" field (Unbound_var -> (apropos ...), bad-args on a prim -> (function-doc ...)) so discovery arrives at the moment it is wanted. A (help) prim renders a short start-here from the existing Special_forms catalog for anyone who never read /agent.txt.")
  ; status partial 2026-09-23: bumpers M5 (loop 7; docs/plans/bumpers-m1-notices.md M5) shipped the
  ; reference half: effect badges + arity ("name (args) [read-db]") in describe single-binding,
  ; package listing, and apropos fallback (image/lib/agent_tools.ml binding_annot / lambda_params,
  ; which reads BOTH the constructor Lambda body and the reader App-lambda form the boot seed and
  ; load-library persist); the doc-coverage footer "documented X/Y — (docs-missing \"pkg\")" on
  ; package describe; and the (docs-missing [pkg]) prim listing visible bindings without docstrings
  ; (function-doc () = missing: store doc, else special-form summary, else prim blurb). These
  ; discovery prims are classified ReadDb in lisp/lib/effect.ml (discovery never writes). bumpers M6
  ; (loop 8; docs/plans/bumpers-m1-notices.md M6) shipped the error-codec hint field
  ; (pure/wire_codec.ml error_hint — known classes only, unknown carry NONE: Unbound_var "a/b" ->
  ; (apropos "a/"), Unbound_var "name" -> (apropos "name"), Prim_error (p,_,"bad-args") ->
  ; (function-doc "p"); additive-only on the existing error envelope, absent for unknown classes).
  ; Still
  ; open to full: the (help) prim.
  (status partial)
  (verify
   (test "describe output for a package includes an effect badge per binding and a doc-coverage footer")
   (test "prim bad-args errors carry a hint field; unknown error classes carry none")
   (smoke "an agent session that has never read agent.txt: (help) -> describe -> docs-missing is a complete orientation loop")))

 (section sequencing
  (doc "M1 notices-envelope + pp_eval -q (wire shape lands additive; nothing else fires yet). M2 define-notices (effects + doc + bench pointer) under the budget rules. M3 route-notices at route_gate (htmx pointer + effects + fragment classification remark). M4 verify-route dry-run. M5 badges in describe/apropos-docs + docs-missing + app-check lint. M6 error hints + durable (agent-profile 'quiet). Each milestone is independently promotable; nothing in the chapter requires the later ones for the earlier to be useful. Habitat-note hygiene: when M2 lands, the feedback half of agent-bowling-bumpers should point here; the note remains the design inbox for the construction half.")
  ; status implemented 2026-09-23: all six milestones shipped across loops 1-8 of
  ; docs/plans/bumpers-m1-notices.md — M1 (wire shape + pp_eval -q), M2 (define-notices), M3
  ; (route-notices), M4 (verify-route), M5 (discovery surfaces), M6 (error hints +
  ; (agent-profile 'quiet) durable quiet). Per invariant, no prim-seam / lisp-interpreter / image
  ; semantics sections were touched by the feedback-half make.
  (status implemented)
  (verify
   (test "borge report shows bumpers progressing per milestone without touching prim-seam/lisp-interpreter/image semantics sections")))

 (section chapter-boundaries
  (doc "Explicit non-homes, so drift has a file to point at: the notices WIRE FIELD is owned by prim-seam's shared-codec section (this chapter defines the concept: field name, dict shape, additive-only rule — prim-seam cites, not re-specs). Eval semantics are in lisp-interpreter and this chapter must not change them — a make here that edits define's return contract is a chapter-boundary bug. Route legality stays in image.borg; this chapter owns only what the advisory NOTICE says. The ui catalog / defpage construction-side scaffolding stays where the habitat note says (ai-authoring / product-experience) — this chapter consumes their existence, never owns it. Tree-time (borge/tree-time.borg) is the record layer beneath staleness notices: bumpers reads gens, it never owns the version model.")
  (status planned)
  (verify
   (test "borge drift after any bumpers make: lisp-interpreter define semantics sections untouched")))
)
